@meta
id: 28-06
title: Two spaces, and nothing else
chapter: 28
type: lesson
skills: vimrc-structure
par: 151
minutes: 6
vscode: editor.tabSize, editor.insertSpaces, and hoping the team agreed
open: .vimrc order.repository.ts
vimrc: set expandtab | set tabstop=2 | set shiftwidth=2 | set autoindent

@brief
Four settings decide what your Tab key and your `>>` actually do, and
they are separate on purpose.

    set expandtab             " Tab inserts spaces, never a tab character
    set tabstop=2             " an existing tab character displays 2 wide
    set shiftwidth=2          " >> and << move by 2

`autoindent` is the fourth and is already in the file: it makes `o` and
Enter keep the current line's indent, which is most of what you want from
an editor that indents.

Add the three missing ones to the Indentation section. Then use them in
the repository buffer, which needs two things:

  1. the `select` object needs `customerId: true,` and `total: true,`
     added under `id: true,`
  2. the one-line body of `count` is flush with its own signature and
     should be one level in

You will not type a single space for either. `o` inherits the indent and
`>>` supplies exactly `shiftwidth` of it.

@keys
| `o` | open below, keeping the indent |
| `>>` | shift the line right by `shiftwidth` |
| `<<` | shift it back |
| `:bn` | next buffer |
| `:wqa` | write everything and quit |

@hints
Config first, `:w`, then `:bn`.
--
Search for `id: true`, then `o` twice — or `o` once and Enter, which is
one keystroke cheaper and keeps both lines at the same indent.
--
For `count`, put the cursor anywhere on the `return` line and press `>>`
once. `shiftwidth=2` means one press is one level.

@solution
    /set autoindent<Enter> o set expandtab<Enter> set tabstop=2<Enter>
    set shiftwidth=2 <Esc> :w<Enter>
    :bn<Enter> /id: true<Enter> o customerId: true,<Enter> total: true, <Esc>
    /return this<Enter> >> :wqa<Enter>

Nothing in the solution types leading whitespace, and that is the test of
whether these settings are right. If you ever find yourself pressing
space four times at the start of a line, one of `expandtab`,
`shiftwidth` or `autoindent` is not what you think it is, and
`:verbose set shiftwidth?` will tell you who last touched it.

Why `tabstop` and `shiftwidth` are different numbers in many configs:
`tabstop` is only about *displaying* a tab character that is already in
the file — a Makefile, a Go file, someone else's commit. `shiftwidth` is
about the indentation you produce. Setting `tabstop=8` and
`shiftwidth=2` is a perfectly coherent position: show real tabs the way
the rest of the world does, and never write one yourself.

There is one more of these, `softtabstop`, which controls what a single
Tab press inserts when it differs from `shiftwidth`. Add it the day it
annoys you, and not before.

@start .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Indentation ---
set autoindent

@expected .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Indentation ---
set autoindent
set expandtab
set tabstop=2
set shiftwidth=2

@start order.repository.ts
export class OrderRepository {
  public async findOne(args: FindOneArgs) {
    return this.prisma.order.findFirst({
      where: args.where,
      select: {
        id: true,
      },
    });
  }

  public async count(args: CountArgs) {
  return this.prisma.order.count({ where: args.where });
  }
}

@expected order.repository.ts
export class OrderRepository {
  public async findOne(args: FindOneArgs) {
    return this.prisma.order.findFirst({
      where: args.where,
      select: {
        id: true,
        customerId: true,
        total: true,
      },
    });
  }

  public async count(args: CountArgs) {
    return this.prisma.order.count({ where: args.where });
  }
}
